The powerdomain construction sends an approximable map to an approximable map, preserving identity and composition, and the result is computable.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`PFmap`).** For an approximable map from one neighbourhood system to another, define its powerdomain action by relating two finite upper families when every source neighbourhood has a related target neighbourhood in the target family.

**Theorem (`PFmap_rel`).** Two powerdomain neighbourhoods are related by the lifted map exactly when they are valid powerdomain neighbourhoods and every member of the first has a related member of the second.

*Proof.* This is exactly the relation used to define the powerdomain action. ∎

**Definition (`PFmapComb`).** Define the higher-order powerdomain operator that sends each neighbourhood of approximable maps to a neighbourhood containing every corresponding lifted map.

**Theorem (`PFmapComb_rel`).** A function-space neighbourhood and a lifted function-space neighbourhood are related exactly when every map in the former has its powerdomain lifting in the latter.

*Proof.* The equivalence is the defining condition of the higher-order relation. ∎

**Theorem (`PDmem_mem`).** Every member of a powerdomain neighbourhood is itself a neighbourhood of the underlying system.

*Proof.* A powerdomain neighbourhood is a finite union of principal upper families generated by neighbourhoods. Membership therefore supplies a generator contained in the given member, and upward closure makes that member a neighbourhood. ∎

**Theorem (`PFmap_idMap`).** Lifting the identity approximable map to powerdomains gives the identity map on the powerdomain.

*Proof.* If every source member is related by the identity to a target member, then each source member lies in the target family by downward closure. Conversely, inclusion of the source family in the target family lets each member witness itself. ∎

**Theorem (`PFmap_rel_Ypd_iff`).** For presented powerdomain neighbourhoods, the lifted relation holds exactly when every decoded source index has a decoded target index whose basic neighbourhoods are related.

*Proof.* In the forward direction, apply the lifted relation to each basic source neighbourhood and choose a basic target neighbourhood below the resulting member. In the reverse direction, choose basic generators below arbitrary family members and use monotonicity of the original map. ∎

**Theorem (`PFmap_rel_fin`).** For finite upper families generated by two lists, the lifted relation holds exactly when every generator in the source list has a related generator in the target list.

*Proof.* Applying the lifted relation to a source generator yields a target-family member, hence a target generator below it; codomain monotonicity gives the required generator relation. Conversely, choose the stipulated target generator below each arbitrary source-family member and use domain monotonicity. ∎

**Theorem (`bExists_decodeList_re`).** If a binary relation is recursively enumerable, then the relation saying that some member of a decoded finite list is related to a given number is recursively enumerable.

*Proof.* Membership in a decoded finite list is decidable. Conjoining it with the given enumerable relation and projecting away the candidate witness preserves recursive enumerability. ∎

**Theorem (`PFmap_isComputable_Ypd`).** On the canonical enumerations of powerdomain neighbourhoods, the relation induced by the lifted map is recursively enumerable.

*Proof.* The finite-code characterization reduces the relation to a bounded universal quantifier over source indices followed by a bounded existential quantifier over target indices. Recursive enumerability is preserved by both finite quantifiers and by exchanging the paired inputs. ∎

**Theorem (`PFmap_isComputable`).** The powerdomain lifting of a computable approximable map is computable for the induced computable powerdomain presentations.

*Proof.* Computability for these presentations is precisely recursive enumerability on their canonical coded neighbourhoods, supplied by the preceding theorem. ∎

**Theorem (`comp_witness`).** Given finitely many source neighbourhoods whose composites reach a target family, one can choose a finite intermediate family witnessing both stages of the composite.

*Proof.* Induct on the source list. The empty list uses no intermediates. For a new head, choose one of its intermediate witnesses, prepend it to the list obtained for the tail, and retain the corresponding witnesses for both stages. ∎

**Theorem (`PFmap_comp`).** Powerdomain lifting preserves composition of approximable maps.

*Proof.* For the forward implication, express the source family by finitely many generators and collect finitely many intermediate witnesses; their generated upper family witnesses the composite of the two lifted maps. For the reverse implication, compose the two witnesses chosen successively for each source member. ∎

**Theorem (`gather_witnesses`).** Finite pointwise existential witnesses can be collected into a finite list of related source–target pairs, with at least one pair for each source.

*Proof.* Induct on the source list, choosing one target witness for the head and adjoining that pair to the collection furnished for the tail. ∎

**Theorem (`exists_stepFun_forall_PFmap_rel`).** Any finite family of lifted constraints satisfied by a map is forced by finitely many ordinary step constraints satisfied by that map.

*Proof.* For one lifted constraint, write its two powerdomain neighbourhoods using finite generators and collect one related pair for each source generator. For a finite list of constraints, append these pairs to the pairs obtained inductively; any map satisfying all collected pairs satisfies each lifted constraint by the finite-generator characterization. ∎

**Theorem (`PFmapComb_toElementMap`).** On elements, the higher-order lifting operator sends the filter of a map to the filter of its powerdomain lifting.

*Proof.* One inclusion applies the defining universal condition to the given map. For the other, each finite neighbourhood of the lifted map is forced by finitely many step constraints; the function-space neighbourhood generated by those constraints contains the original map and is sent inside the chosen lifted neighbourhood. ∎
